---
/**
 * THE SOUND SNIPPET, SHARED BY BOTH LAYOUTS.
 *
 * `../sound/interface-sound.js` is the single implementation and this file is how it reaches a
 * page. Base.astro had a copy of a sound system inside its module script and the landing had none
 * at all, which is the shape a feature takes just before the two versions stop agreeing — so there
 * is now one file, imported as text and inlined, and two layouts that render this component.
 *
 * WHY `?raw` AND `is:inline` RATHER THAN A BUNDLED MODULE. The landing's whole point is that it
 * ships essentially nothing; a hoisted module would be a bundle request for forty lines of synth.
 * Inlining also removes a failure this repository has already had once today: Astro hoists a
 * component <script> into a module script, `document.currentScript` is null there by specification,
 * and a hero effect that relied on it never ran at all. An inline script at the end of <body> runs
 * where it is written, against a DOM that is already parsed.
 *
 * THE CONTROL IS A SEPARATE COMPONENT — SoundDock.astro — and only the landing renders it, because
 * the chrome routes already carry a toggle in Nav.astro. Keeping the markup and its stylesheet out
 * of this file is what stops eighteen routes shipping the CSS for an element they do not have.
 */
import SOURCE from '../sound/interface-sound.js?raw';
---

<script is:inline set:html={SOURCE}></script>
